Title: Point the npm package at this project's releases
Origin: electron-legacy (this repo)

src/electron/npm/ is the source of the `electron` npm package: index.js, cli.js,
install.js and a package.json that upstream publishes with name, version and the
rest injected at release time (there is no `npm publish` in the Electron repo
any more; that moved to external tooling). build.sh --npm assembles npm_package/
from these files, so customising the package means patching them here rather
than hand-maintaining a copy.

  npm/install.js    downloadArtifact() gains mirrorOptions. @electron/get
                    otherwise resolves
                      https://github.com/electron/electron/releases/download/
                    and a consumer would get upstream's binaries from a package
                    claiming to be ours. The URL it builds is
                      ${mirror}v${version}/electron-v${version}-${platform}-${arch}.zip
                    which is exactly the layout package() writes into dist/, so
                    uploading dist/v<version>/*.zip as release assets on the
                    matching tag is all it takes to serve it. mirrorVar() in
                    @electron/get reads env and npm config *before* the value
                    passed here, so ELECTRON_MIRROR and
                    npm_config_electron_mirror still override it.

  npm/package.json  the static half of what upstream injects at publish time:
                    name, description, repository, license, author, keywords.
                    Deliberately not version -- install.js reads that to build
                    the download URL, so it has to track ELECTRON_VERSION, and
                    --npm writes it when it assembles the package. Keeping the
                    name as "electron" is what lets this drop into a
                    package.json as a replacement: require('electron'), the
                    electron bin, and anything resolving the electron package
                    all keep working.

Checksum verification is left on. upstream's install.js reads ./checksums.json,
and --npm generates it from the SHASUMS256.txt that package() already writes,
so there is no reason to disable it.

diff --git a/electron/npm/install.js b/electron/npm/install.js
index 1d0f702..b9abbd7 100644
--- a/electron/npm/install.js
+++ b/electron/npm/install.js
@@ -9,6 +9,14 @@
 
 const { version } = require('./package');
 
+// Where the release zips live. @electron/get builds
+// `${mirror}v${version}/electron-v${version}-${platform}-${arch}.zip`, which is
+// the layout build.sh's package() writes into dist/ and that these releases are
+// uploaded with. Env and npm config are read ahead of this value (see
+// mirrorVar() in @electron/get), so a consumer can redirect it with
+// ELECTRON_MIRROR or npm_config_electron_mirror without editing anything.
+const LEGACY_MIRROR = 'https://github.com/Alex313031/electron-legacy/releases/download/';
+
 const platformPath = getPlatformPath();
 
 if (isInstalled()) {
@@ -40,6 +48,7 @@
 downloadArtifact({
   version,
   artifactName: 'electron',
+  mirrorOptions: { mirror: LEGACY_MIRROR },
   force: process.env.force_no_cache === 'true',
   cacheRoot: process.env.electron_config_cache,
   checksums:
